// ==========================================================================
// Entry (contract interface):
//
// Methods the framework calls although no code in the application program does.
// ==========================================================================




// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Relation
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A method the framework calls, without a call to it in the application program.
// Its `this` is either:
//   - the mock object for its class when the class is an ExternallyCreatedType;
//   - the objects the program creates of its class when the framework injects into them
//     (e.g. objects returned from Spring @Bean methods get their @PostConstruct called)
//     or when nobody outside the program creates the class 
//     (e.g. a HandlerInterceptor the program creates with new and registers in code).
// Its parameters get what InjectedWith says, or a mock of their type when
// not known (e.g. a request body).
// Spring: @GetMapping, @PostMapping, @Scheduled, @EventListener etc...
// Jakarta: @GET, @POST, @PostConstruct etc...
.decl EntryPoint(?owner:symbol, ?method:Method)







// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tuples the contract adds
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Always true when another tuple exists, whatever the framework, so specs and frameworks
// don't need to repeat or ever forget them. Each belongs to the owner of the tuple it follows from
// (for a request its whoever injects into the object).


// Only the caller of a method passes its arguments.
// So if the framework fills a parameter, it also calls the method.
EntryPoint(?owner, ?method) :-
  InjectedParam(?owner, ?param),
  FormalParam(_, ?method, ?param).


// The framework calls a method to get what it puts into a slot.
EntryPoint(?owner, ?method) :-
  InjectedWith(?owner, _, _, ?method),
  isMethod(?method).


// Whoever injects into an object calls the methods the program asked for (CallRequest, e.g. @PostConstruct),
// whichever spec or framework wrote the request.
// The tuple is the injecting framework's, the request only says why.
// It's the method a call on the object runs, so an override without the annotation runs instead.
EntryPoint(?framework, ?run) :-
  InjectionTarget(?framework, ?class),
  CallRequest(_, ?method),
  MethodRunOn(?method, ?class, ?run).







// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Checks
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------

ContractProblem(?owner, "EntryPoint", ?method, "Owner not declared") :-
  EntryPoint(?owner, ?method),
  !Owner(?owner).

ContractProblem(?owner, "EntryPoint", ?method, "Not a method") :-
  EntryPoint(?owner, ?method),
  !isMethod(?method).
